---
// DUCKING — a mixing desk for the world's attention (2026-11-01; docs/design/2026-11-01-ducking.md).
//
// The desk comes first and the page explains it afterwards, because the point of it is in the hand
// and the ear. Every number below is derived at build time by src/lib/ducking/* from the round and
// the committed ledger, and none is typed into a sentence. What is typed is the readings, which are
// the figures of the map and the briefing in spoken form, each with its sources.
import SheetHead from '@/components/experiments/SheetHead.astro'
import SheetFoot from '@/components/experiments/SheetFoot.astro'
import TableFallback from '@/components/dataviz/TableFallback.astro'
import DuckingDesk from './DuckingDesk'
import cycle from '@/data/ecology/cycle.json'
import { readDesk } from '@/lib/ducking/desk-data'
import { roundBehind, type CycleState } from '@/lib/ducking/round'
import { fmtCount, fmtShare, toDb, WINDOW_DAYS } from '@/lib/ducking/mix'
import '@/styles/experiment-sheet.css'
import '@/styles/ducking.css'

const REPO = 'https://github.com/frankbueltge/frankbueltge.de'
const link = 'underline decoration-line underline-offset-4 hover:decoration-fg'

const desk = readDesk()
const { round, week, whole, latest, sources, shares, totals, ledger, attention, receiptsShown, book } = desk
const fields = round.channels.length
const ai = week.counts[round.channels.findIndex((c) => c.id === 'ai')]! / week.total
const behind = roundBehind(round.id, cycle as CycleState)
const last = ledger.days.length - 1
const deskChannels = [
  ...round.channels.map(({ id, strip, title }) => ({ id, strip, title })),
  { id: 'rest', strip: round.rest.strip, title: 'The crowd: everything else on the charts' },
]

const receiptsOf = new Map(attention.matters.map((m) => [m.matter, m]))
const rows = round.channels.map((channel, c) => {
  const share = whole.total ? whole.counts[c]! / whole.total : 0
  const db = toDb(share)
  return {
    channel: channel.title,
    week: fmtCount(week.counts[c]!),
    whole: fmtCount(whole.counts[c]!),
    share: fmtShare(share),
    level: Number.isFinite(db) ? `${db.toFixed(1).replace('-', '−')} dB` : '−∞',
  }
})
---

<main id="main" class="sheet mx-auto w-full max-w-5xl flex-1 px-4 py-10 sm:py-14">
  <SheetHead
    kicker="From the counter-measurement line"
    title="Ducking"
    sub="A mixing desk for the world’s attention. What sits low cannot be heard, and it stays up only while you hold it."
    badges={['LIVE DATA', `round ${round.id}`]}
  />

  <section class="mb-12" aria-label="The desk">
    <DuckingDesk
      client:load
      round={round.id}
      channels={deskChannels}
      days={ledger.days}
      shares={shares}
      totals={totals}
      windowDays={WINDOW_DAYS}
    />
    <noscript>
      <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-fg-muted">The desk needs JavaScript to move and to sound. The record and every reading are below.</p>
    </noscript>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-12 max-w-3xl">
    <p class="kicker mb-3">What you are hearing</p>
    <p class="leading-relaxed text-fg-muted">
      Ducking is what a mixing desk does when one signal pushes the others down. This desk seats the {fields} standing fields of
      the ecology’s <a class={link} href="https://github.com/frankbueltge/research-ecology/blob/main/briefings/MAP.md">map</a>, from
      war to AI, each with a fader and a voice reading where the field stands and what the
      <a class={link} href={round.briefing.url}>briefing of {round.briefing.written}</a> says presses in it. The last channel is the
      crowd: everything else on the web’s charts, the football, film stars, apps and code that no field named, read aloud by
      several voices at once from a sample of the entries themselves. All of them run
      all the time, heard or not. A fader stands at its channel’s share of the last {WINDOW_DAYS} days of
      <a class={link} href="/trending">Common Ground</a>, so the first thing you hear is the crowd drowning out everything else.
      That is the measurement.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-fg-muted">
      Push a fader up and hold it. The whole stays a hundred percent, so the room comes from the crowd, and the voice walks out
      of it towards you. The higher you push, the clearer it gets. Two thumbs can hold two fields. Let go and the fader falls back to where the week
      put it. Nothing on this desk stays where you leave it.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-fg-muted">
      Press play and the desk runs back through the record, {book.record.day} seconds a day from {book.record.from}. The faders move
      as the weeks moved them, and the record speaks: real headlines that named a field, each on about the day it was counted, read
      by that field’s voice. A field gets as many of them as its share of those weeks gave it, so you hear it by how often it speaks.
      The crowd is held back for this, because under it you could not hear anything change. It is the one thing on this desk that a
      machine holds for you, and it lets go when the record ends or when your hand touches a fader.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-12 max-w-3xl">
    <p class="kicker mb-3">What the record shows</p>
    <p class="leading-relaxed text-fg-muted">
      In the {WINDOW_DAYS} days to {latest}, Common Ground logged <b class="tabular-nums text-fg">{fmtCount(week.total)}</b> signals
      from {sources} charts and platforms. <b class="tabular-nums text-fg">{fmtCount(week.named)}</b> of them named one of these
      fields, which puts all {fields} together at <b class="tabular-nums text-fg">{fmtShare(week.share)}</b> of the mix. AI alone
      holds <b class="tabular-nums text-fg">{fmtShare(ai)}</b>, and the other {fields - 1} together hold
      <b class="tabular-nums text-fg">{fmtShare(week.share - ai)}</b>.
      {week.silent > 0 && <>{week.silent} of the {fields} had no signal at all this week, and their faders are on the floor. </>}
      Over the whole record, {ledger.days.length} days, the {fields} hold {fmtShare(whole.share)}.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-fg-muted">
      The terms that count a field are wide on purpose, and the receipts below show what they let in that does not belong.
      Narrowing them could only lower the faders.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-12 max-w-3xl">
    <p class="kicker mb-3">Why the sum is fixed</p>
    <p class="leading-relaxed text-fg-muted">
      The crowd is whatever no field named, so the hundred percent of this desk is not a setting: it is the whole
      ledger. That the channels add up is arithmetic, true of any shares, and it proves nothing. That attention outside this
      page behaves the same way is a claim, and there is evidence for it. Jian-Hua Zhu called agenda-setting
      <a class={link} href="https://scholars.cityu.edu.hk/en/publications/issue-competition-and-attention-distraction-a-zero-sum-theory-of-">a zero-sum process</a>
      in 1992. Thomas Eisensee and David Strömberg followed about 5,000 natural disasters from 1968 to 2002 and found that
      <a class={link} href="https://doi.org/10.1162/qjec.122.2.693">US relief depended on what else was in the news</a>: at the
      highest news pressure, a disaster needed six times the casualties for the same chance of relief. Matthew Sisco and
      colleagues draw the limit: as concern about COVID-19 grew,
      <a class={link} href="https://collaborate.princeton.edu/en/publications/examining-evidence-for-the-finite-pool-of-worry-and-finite-pool-o">attention to climate fell and worry about it did not</a>.
      What runs out, on that reading, is attention, not care.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-fg-muted">
      Try it the other way round. Pull <b class="text-fg">{round.rest.strip}</b> down and hold it. The room it frees goes to
      the fields in the proportions they already have, so the loud get louder. A channel at zero stays at zero. Attention
      that comes free does not go to what was ignored; only a hand lifts that.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-12 max-w-3xl">
    <p class="kicker mb-3">How to read a fader</p>
    <p class="leading-relaxed text-fg-muted">
      The scale is logarithmic, with the top of the travel stretched, as on any desk, because that is where a channel can be
      heard. Each mark is ten times the one below, and the numbers are percent. A channel’s share is its share of the power of
      the mix: a field at one percent stands twenty decibels under the whole. That is why the lower part of a fader’s travel is
      silent. One thing cannot be read off a fader: as your hand raises a field, the crowd also blurs, the way a room does when
      you listen closely to one person.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-12 max-w-3xl">
    <p class="kicker mb-3">What the voices read</p>
    <p class="mb-3 leading-relaxed text-fg-muted">
      Every voice here is synthetic, made with an open model on one machine. Each reads where its field stands, from the map, then
      the briefing’s matters in that field. Every figure was checked against its source on {round.checked}, and where the briefing
      was out of date or wrong, the voice says what the source says. The faders move every day; the words stay until the next round
      is recorded.
      {behind && <b class="text-fg">The ecology has written a newer briefing since. These are still the words of {round.briefing.written}.</b>}
    </p>
    {
      round.channels.map((channel) => (
        <article class="du-reading">
          <h3>{channel.title}</h3>
          <p class="leading-relaxed text-fg-muted">{channel.lines.map((l) => l.text).join(' ')}</p>
          <p class="mt-1 font-mono text-mono-sm text-fg-faint">
            {channel.sources.map((s, i) => (
              <>
                {i > 0 && ' · '}
                <a class={link} href={s.url}>{s.label}</a>
              </>
            ))}
          </p>
        </article>
      ))
    }
  </section>

  <section class="mb-12 max-w-3xl">
    <p class="kicker mb-3">The record</p>
    <TableFallback
      summary="The desk as a table"
      open
      caption="Signals naming each field, this week and over the whole record, with the share and level of the whole record"
      columns={[
        { key: 'channel', label: 'Field' },
        { key: 'week', label: `Last ${WINDOW_DAYS} days`, align: 'right', nowrap: true },
        { key: 'whole', label: 'Whole record', align: 'right', nowrap: true },
        { key: 'share', label: 'Share', align: 'right', nowrap: true },
        { key: 'level', label: 'Level', align: 'right', nowrap: true },
      ]}
      rows={rows}
    />
    <details class="mt-4">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer text-fg-muted">What was counted: the newest signals per field</summary>
      {
        round.channels.map((channel) => {
          const counted = receiptsOf.get(channel.id)
          return (
            <div class="mt-4">
              <h3 class="font-semibold">{channel.strip}</h3>
              <p class="font-mono text-mono-sm text-fg-faint">{channel.terms.join(' · ')}</p>
              {counted && counted.receipts.length > 0 ? (
                <ul class="mt-1 list-disc space-y-0.5 pl-5 text-sm text-fg-muted">
                  {counted.receipts.slice(0, receiptsShown).map((r) => (
                    <li>
                      {r.url ? <a class={link} href={r.url}>{r.label}</a> : r.label}{' '}
                      <span class="font-mono text-mono-sm text-fg-faint">{r.source}, {r.date}</span>
                    </li>
                  ))}
                </ul>
              ) : (
                <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-fg-muted">No signal in the whole record.</p>
              )}
            </div>
          )
        })
      }
    </details>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-12 max-w-3xl">
    <p class="kicker mb-3">What this cannot say</p>
    <ul class="list-disc space-y-2 pl-5 leading-relaxed text-fg-muted">
      <li><b class="text-fg">A chart is not a mind.</b> The desk reads the tops of public charts and feeds, leaning to technology
        and to English. It says what those charts held, never what anyone cares about. A matter can be everything to the people
        living it and still sit on this floor.</li>
      <li><b class="text-fg">AI stands where it stands partly because of who is counted.</b> More than a quarter of the ledger’s
        signals come from developer platforms. A field’s fader is its place on these charts, and not its place in the world.</li>
      <li><b class="text-fg">The fields are one map’s.</b> Another reading of the world would seat other channels, and the crowd
        would hold what they left.</li>
      <li><b class="text-fg">A week is a choice.</b> So is a term. Both move a fader.</li>
      <li><b class="text-fg">The record has a step in it.</b> From 2026-11-02 the ledger reads ten editions of Google News instead
        of one, so a field can rise on that day without anyone having turned to it.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <SheetFoot links={[
    { href: '/werke/ducking', label: 'method sheet' },
    { href: `${REPO}/blob/main/src/data/ducking/rounds/${round.id}.json`, label: 'the round', external: true },
    { href: `${REPO}/tree/main/src/data/trending`, label: 'the ledger', external: true },
    { href: '/third-reading', label: 'a reading you can give' },
    { href: '/experiments/neighbors', label: 'neighbours' },
    { href: '/experiments', label: 'the lab' },
  ]}>
    <p class="max-w-2xl font-mono text-mono-sm leading-relaxed text-fg-faint">
      {ledger.days.length} days, {ledger.days[0]} to {ledger.days[last]}, {fmtCount(whole.total)} signals, read from the committed
      day files of Common Ground at build time. Open data, CC0. Count: <code>src/lib/ducking/mix.ts</code>. Every level and count on
      this page is derived from those files; the figures in the readings are the briefing’s.
    </p>
  </SheetFoot>
</main>
